Lab Tasks
Task 1: Deploy a Microservices Application
In this task, you will deploy a sample bookstore application consisting of multiple microservices that will communicate through the service mesh.

Subtask 1.1: Create Project and Enable Service Mesh
First, create a new project for your microservices application and add it to the service mesh.

Create a new project:
oc new-project bookstore-mesh
Label the namespace for automatic sidecar injection:
oc label namespace bookstore-mesh istio-injection=enabled
Verify the namespace is labeled correctly:
oc get namespace bookstore-mesh --show-labels
You should see istio-injection=enabled in the labels output.

Subtask 1.2: Deploy the Product Catalog Service
Deploy the first microservice that will serve as the product catalog.

Create the product catalog deployment:
cat << 'EOF' > productcatalog-deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: productcatalog
  labels:
    app: productcatalog
    version: v1
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: productcatalog
      version: v1
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: productcatalog
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: productcatalog
        image: quay.io/redhattraining/ossm-productcatalog:1.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
        env:
        - name: PORT
          value: "8080"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: productcatalog
  labels:
    app: productcatalog
spec:
  ports:
  - port: 8080
    name: http
  selector:
    app: productcatalog
EOF
Apply the product catalog configuration:
oc apply -f productcatalog-deployment.yaml
Verify the deployment:
oc get pods -l app=productcatalog
Subtask 1.3: Deploy the Reviews Service
Deploy the reviews microservice that will communicate with the product catalog.

Create the reviews service deployment:
cat << 'EOF' > reviews-deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: reviews-v1
  labels:
    app: reviews
    version: v1
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: reviews
      version: v1
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: reviews
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: reviews
        image: quay.io/redhattraining/ossm-reviews:1.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9080
        env:
        - name: PRODUCTCATALOG_SERVICE
          value: "productcatalog:8080"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: reviews
  labels:
    app: reviews
spec:
  ports:
  - port: 9080
    name: http
  selector:
    app: reviews
EOF
Apply the reviews service configuration:
oc apply -f reviews-deployment.yaml
Verify both services are running:
oc get pods
Subtask 1.4: Deploy the Frontend Service
Deploy the frontend service that will serve as the entry point to your microservices application.

Create the frontend deployment:
cat << 'EOF' > frontend-deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend
  labels:
    app: frontend
    version: v1
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend
      version: v1
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: frontend
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: frontend
        image: quay.io/redhattraining/ossm-frontend:1.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
        env:
        - name: REVIEWS_SERVICE
          value: "reviews:9080"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: frontend
  labels:
    app: frontend
spec:
  ports:
  - port: 8080
    name: http
  selector:
    app: frontend
EOF
Apply the frontend configuration:
oc apply -f frontend-deployment.yaml
Verify all services are deployed:
oc get svc
Task 2: Confirm Sidecar Proxy Injection
In this task, you will verify that Istio has automatically injected sidecar proxies into your microservices pods.

Subtask 2.1: Examine Pod Configuration
Check that each pod now contains both the application container and the Istio sidecar proxy.

List all pods with detailed information:
oc get pods -o wide
Check the number of containers in each pod:
oc get pods -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.containers[*].name}{"\n"}{end}'
You should see that each pod has two containers: your application container and the istio-proxy container.

Subtask 2.2: Inspect Sidecar Proxy Details
Examine the sidecar proxy configuration in detail.

Describe a pod to see the sidecar proxy:
POD_NAME=$(oc get pods -l app=frontend -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
oc describe pod $POD_NAME
Check the Istio proxy logs:
oc logs $POD_NAME -c istio-proxy
Verify the Envoy admin interface is accessible:
oc exec $POD_NAME -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/stats | head -10
Subtask 2.3: Validate Proxy Configuration
Use istioctl to validate the proxy configuration.

Check proxy configuration status:
istioctl proxy-status
Get detailed proxy configuration for a specific pod:
istioctl proxy-config cluster $POD_NAME.bookstore-mesh
Task 3: Test Service-to-Service Communication via OpenShift Service Mesh
In this task, you will test communication between your microservices and verify that traffic flows through the service mesh.

Subtask 3.1: Create Gateway and Virtual Service
Configure Istio Gateway and VirtualService to expose your application.

Create the Gateway configuration:
cat << 'EOF' > bookstore-gateway.yaml
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: bookstore-gateway
spec:
  selector:
    istio: ingressgateway
  servers:
  - port:
      number: 80
      name: http
      protocol: HTTP
    hosts:
    - "*"
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: bookstore-vs
spec:
  hosts:
  - "*"
  gateways:
  - bookstore-gateway
  http:
  - match:
    - uri:
        prefix: /
    route:
    - destination:
        host: frontend
        port:
          number: 8080
EOF
Apply the Gateway configuration:
oc apply -f bookstore-gateway.yaml
Verify the Gateway is created:
oc get gateway
oc get virtualservice
Subtask 3.2: Test External Access
Test access to your application through the Istio ingress gateway.

Get the ingress gateway URL:
export INGRESS_HOST=$(oc get route istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Application URL: http://$INGRESS_HOST"
Test the application endpoint:
curl -v http://$INGRESS_HOST/
Test multiple requests to generate traffic:
for i in {1..10}; do
  curl -s http://$INGRESS_HOST/ | grep -o "Product Catalog\|Reviews"
  sleep 1
done
Subtask 3.3: Test Internal Service Communication
Verify that services can communicate with each other through the mesh.

Test communication from frontend to reviews service:
FRONTEND_POD=$(oc get pods -l app=frontend -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
oc exec $FRONTEND_POD -c frontend -- curl -s reviews:9080/health
Test communication from reviews to productcatalog service:
REVIEWS_POD=$(oc get pods -l app=reviews -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
oc exec $REVIEWS_POD -c reviews -- curl -s productcatalog:8080/products
Verify service discovery through Envoy:
oc exec $FRONTEND_POD -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/clusters | grep reviews
Subtask 3.4: Monitor Traffic with Kiali
Use Kiali to visualize service mesh traffic.

Access Kiali dashboard:
oc get route kiali -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}'
Generate traffic for visualization:
for i in {1..50}; do
  curl -s http://$INGRESS_HOST/ > /dev/null
  sleep 0.5
done
In the Kiali dashboard:
Navigate to Graph
Select the bookstore-mesh namespace
Choose "Versioned app graph"
Observe the traffic flow between services
Subtask 3.5: Verify Service Mesh Metrics
Check that the service mesh is collecting metrics from your microservices.

View Envoy metrics from a pod:
oc exec $FRONTEND_POD -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/stats | grep http_inbound
Check Prometheus metrics:
PROMETHEUS_URL=$(oc get route prometheus -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
curl -s "http://$PROMETHEUS_URL/api/v1/query?query=istio_requests_total" | jq '.data.result[0].metric'
Verify service mesh telemetry:
istioctl proxy-config bootstrap $FRONTEND_POD.bookstore-mesh | grep telemetry -A 5
Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
Issue: Pods are not getting sidecar injection

Solution: Verify namespace labeling with oc get namespace bookstore-mesh --show-labels
Solution: Check if the ServiceMeshMemberRoll includes your namespace
Issue: Services cannot communicate

Solution: Verify services are in the same namespace or properly configured for cross-namespace communication
Solution: Check NetworkPolicies that might be blocking traffic
Issue: Gateway not accessible

Solution: Verify the istio-ingressgateway service is running: oc get svc -n istio-system
Solution: Check route configuration: oc get route -n istio-system
Issue: Kiali dashboard not showing traffic

Solution: Generate more traffic to make it visible
Solution: Check time range settings in Kiali
Solution: Verify telemetry configuration
Verification Commands
Use these commands to verify your setup:

# Check all pods have 2 containers (app + sidecar)
oc get pods -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.containers[*].name}{"\n"}{end}'

# Verify service mesh configuration
istioctl analyze -n bookstore-mesh

# Check proxy synchronization
istioctl proxy-status

# Test service connectivity
oc exec deployment/frontend -c frontend -- curl -s reviews:9080/health
Conclusion
In this lab, you successfully:

Deployed a multi-tier microservices application consisting of frontend, reviews, and product catalog services within OpenShift Service Mesh
Verified automatic sidecar proxy injection by examining pod configurations and confirming that each pod contains both application and Istio proxy containers
Configured and tested service-to-service communication through the mesh using Istio Gateway and VirtualService resources
Monitored traffic flow using Kiali dashboard and verified that all communication flows through the service mesh proxies
Validated mesh functionality by checking metrics, logs, and proxy configurations
This hands-on experience demonstrates the power of service mesh architecture for microservices. The automatic sidecar injection provides transparent service-to-service communication, security, and observability without requiring changes to your application code. The service mesh handles complex networking concerns like load balancing, circuit breaking, and traffic management, allowing developers to focus on business logic rather than infrastructure concerns.

Understanding these concepts is crucial for the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Building Resilient Microservices exam, as service mesh technology is becoming the standard for managing microservices communication in enterprise environments. The skills you've learned here will help you build more resilient, observable, and secure microservices architectures.
